  about: 'If the Dawn hymn is the most beloved poetry in the Rig Veda, this is its twin — the collection\'s one true hymn to Night, and its opposite in every way except beauty. Where dawn is praised as a radiant young woman flinging open the day, Night arrives here as her own goddess: not darkness to be feared, not the mere absence of light, but a living presence who "puts on all her glories," fills the heights and the depths, and settles the whole world into rest. The two hymns are sisters in the text as Night and Dawn are sisters in it — and reading them together is the whole of the Beyond Outcomes dawn-and-night imagery in its original source. This one is quieter, more intimate: after the cosmic praise it becomes, in its last verses, a small human prayer whispered in the dark — keep the wolf away, keep the thief away, let me pass safely through to morning. It is one of the gentlest things in the Veda: a civilization, at the edge of sleep, asking the night to be kind.',
